WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.050
You probably already
know that when you

2
00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:03.774
create a table in a database,

3
00:00:03.774 --> 00:00:05.830
you must define the
column names and

4
00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:07.150
the data type of the content

5
00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:09.010
that will reside
in those columns.

6
00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:10.750
You can use a string datatype

7
00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.460
to define the columns datatype,

8
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.470
particularly in instances
when it accepts

9
00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:17.065
both numeric and
text characters.

10
00:00:17.065 --> 00:00:20.020
In this video, you'll
explore the string datatype,

11
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.505
and by the end of the video,

12
00:00:21.505 --> 00:00:23.740
you'll be able to explain
the string datatype in

13
00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:25.510
a database and differentiate

14
00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:28.220
between CHARR and
VARCHAR datatypes.

15
00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:30.939
When you create a
table in a database,

16
00:00:30.939 --> 00:00:33.430
it is important for data
integrity to ensure that

17
00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:36.175
only valid values are
inserted in your table.

18
00:00:36.175 --> 00:00:39.400
For example, you should use
string datatype when you

19
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:40.570
intend to store data that

20
00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:42.935
contains a mix of
character types.

21
00:00:42.935 --> 00:00:45.245
If you define a
column as a string,

22
00:00:45.245 --> 00:00:47.390
then any type of text
can be inserted.

23
00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:49.910
This includes
alphabet characters,

24
00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:52.780
numeric characters, and
special characters.

25
00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:54.785
Let's explore an
example to find out

26
00:00:54.785 --> 00:00:57.425
more about how string
datatypes work.

27
00:00:57.425 --> 00:00:58.820
Let's take the example of

28
00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:01.105
a student table from
a college database.

29
00:01:01.105 --> 00:01:03.050
This table stores student login

30
00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:05.705
information for the
colleges online portal.

31
00:01:05.705 --> 00:01:07.220
It stores this information

32
00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:08.945
under the following
four columns;

33
00:01:08.945 --> 00:01:13.885
Student name, username,
password and email address.

34
00:01:13.885 --> 00:01:15.440
The student name column

35
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.675
contains only
alphabet characters.

36
00:01:17.675 --> 00:01:21.050
The username column contains
alphanumeric characters,

37
00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:22.850
and the password
and email column

38
00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:25.055
contains a mix of
character types.

39
00:01:25.055 --> 00:01:27.830
String datatype is
a generic term used

40
00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:30.590
for different string
datatypes in the database.

41
00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:33.425
The most used string
datatypes are CHAR,

42
00:01:33.425 --> 00:01:35.095
which stands for character.

43
00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:37.010
This datatype is used to hold

44
00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:38.825
characters of a fixed length.

45
00:01:38.825 --> 00:01:41.615
VARCHAR stands for
variable character.

46
00:01:41.615 --> 00:01:44.330
This holds characters
of the variable length.

47
00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:46.760
Let's explore these string
data types further.

48
00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.800
CHAR means that the given length

49
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.735
of the characters
is predetermined.

50
00:01:50.735 --> 00:01:53.450
It can't be changed
after declaration.

51
00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:55.820
Column attributes
are defined as CHAR

52
00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:58.250
followed by a character
length in parentheses.

53
00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:00.950
For example, CHAR (50)
means that a column

54
00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:04.135
only permits 50 characters
of space in each field.

55
00:02:04.135 --> 00:02:05.990
CHAR is the best
option if you've

56
00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:08.570
predefined size of character
that you want to maintain.

57
00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:10.610
In the student table,

58
00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:13.010
you can set a maximum
length of 50 characters for

59
00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:16.355
the username column in
SQL with CHAR (50).

60
00:02:16.355 --> 00:02:19.220
For example, the table
contains the records

61
00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.175
for a student with a
username Mark 1 2 3,

62
00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:24.365
which is a total of
seven characters.

63
00:02:24.365 --> 00:02:27.315
However, because the column
is defined as CHAR (50),

64
00:02:27.315 --> 00:02:29.000
this username occupies
the length of

65
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.100
50 characters within the space.

66
00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:34.415
The VARCHAR datatype works
in a similar way to CHAR.

67
00:02:34.415 --> 00:02:36.290
Whoever it is a variable length.

68
00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:38.435
This means that the
length can be changed.

69
00:02:38.435 --> 00:02:41.480
It's not fixed. VARCHAR
are often used when you're

70
00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:42.830
not sure how many characters

71
00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:44.855
might be inserted in
the column field.

72
00:02:44.855 --> 00:02:47.600
You can type
VARCHAR(50) in SQL to

73
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.995
allow for any input up to
maximum of 50 characters.

74
00:02:50.995 --> 00:02:52.790
In the student table example,

75
00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:54.470
the student name
column would most

76
00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:56.720
likely contain names
of varying length.

77
00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.910
You could define the
student name column

78
00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:01.175
as VARCHAR(50) in SQL

79
00:03:01.175 --> 00:03:03.620
This means that the name
of each student only

80
00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:04.985
occupies as much space

81
00:03:04.985 --> 00:03:06.905
as there are characters
in their name.

82
00:03:06.905 --> 00:03:08.570
For example, Mark Simpson

83
00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:11.045
occupies far less
than 50 characters.

84
00:03:11.045 --> 00:03:12.920
But this field could
hold a name up to

85
00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.350
the value of 50
characters if required.

86
00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:17.390
Finally, let's briefly explore

87
00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:20.435
some more commonly used
examples of string datatypes.

88
00:03:20.435 --> 00:03:23.720
TINYTEXT is used to define
columns that require

89
00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:27.505
less than 255 characters,
like short paragraphs.

90
00:03:27.505 --> 00:03:30.080
TEXT is used to define
columns of less than

91
00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.695
65,000 characters,
like an article.

92
00:03:32.695 --> 00:03:37.055
MEDIUMTEXT defined columns
of 16.7 million characters.

93
00:03:37.055 --> 00:03:38.915
For example, the text of a book.

94
00:03:38.915 --> 00:03:41.150
The LONGTEXT datatype
stores up to

95
00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:43.735
four gigabytes of text data.

96
00:03:43.735 --> 00:03:45.710
You should now be
able to explain

97
00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:47.990
the string datatype as
used in a database.

98
00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:50.390
You should also be capable
of differentiating between

99
00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:55.650
string datatypes including
CHAR and VARCHAR. Great work.